WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:14.443
A few weeks later, spring arrived.

2
00:00:15.304 --> 00:00:18.465
Every tree and flower put forth young leaves and rich blossoms.

3
00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:22.446
The Maylies and Oliver left Chertsey for a cottage in the country.

4
00:00:23.526 --> 00:00:24.546
It was a happy time.

5
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:29.368
Every morning Oliver was up by six o'clock, roaming the fields in search of flowers.

6
00:00:30.516 --> 00:00:33.218
He returned home laden with blossoms for the breakfast table.

7
00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:37.180
Then he went to the village to study with a kind, white-haired gentleman.

8
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.881
In the afternoon he walked with Mrs.

9
00:00:39.921 --> 00:00:40.722
Maylie and Rose.

10
00:00:41.622 --> 00:00:44.664
Sometimes they sat in a shady place and listened to Rose read aloud.

11
00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:48.026
After that Oliver prepared his lesson for the next day.

12
00:00:49.026 --> 00:00:53.408
In the evening they walked out again, and Oliver listened with great pleasure to all that the ladies

13
00:00:53.468 --> 00:00:59.692
said. When darkness fell and they returned home, Rose played the piano and sang in a gentle voice.

14
00:01:01.512 --> 00:01:04.495
So three months glided away and spring melted into summer.

15
00:01:05.736 --> 00:01:08.278
One beautiful night they had taken a longer walk than usual.

16
00:01:09.138 --> 00:01:11.480
When they returned home, Rose sat down at the piano.

17
00:01:12.621 --> 00:01:15.944
After running her hands over the keys, she began to play something solemn.

18
00:01:16.925 --> 00:01:18.926
And as she played, Oliver and Mrs.

19
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:20.087
Maylie heard her weeping.

20
00:01:20.107 --> 00:01:22.289
"Rose, my love!"

21
00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:23.150
cried Mrs.

22
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.291
Maylie, rising hastily and bending over her.

23
00:01:25.312 --> 00:01:27.553
"Why are you crying?"

24
00:01:27.573 --> 00:01:30.556
"I don't know," replied the young lady.

25
00:01:31.484 --> 00:01:35.846
"I can't describe it, but I feel "Not ill, my love?"

26
00:01:35.866 --> 00:01:37.867
"No, not ill."

27
00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:39.967
Rose shuddered with cold.

28
00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:41.788
"I'll be better soon.

29
00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:43.689
Please close the window."

30
00:01:44.809 --> 00:01:46.790
Oliver hurried to comply with her request.

31
00:01:47.811 --> 00:01:51.632
Rose tried to play a livelier tune, but her fingers fell powerless on the keys.

32
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:56.854
Covering her face with her hands, she sank onto a sofa and wept.

33
00:01:56.874 --> 00:02:00.936
"I don't want to alarm you, Aunt, but I fear I am ill."

34
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.823
Her face turned as white as marble, yet in another minute, it was flushed.

35
00:02:07.044 --> 00:02:08.585
Oliver was alarmed, but Mrs.

36
00:02:08.645 --> 00:02:09.906
Maylie tried to stay calm.

37
00:02:10.946 --> 00:02:12.768
She urged Rose to retire for the night.

38
00:02:12.788 --> 00:02:17.231
"I hope nothing is the matter," said Oliver when Rose had left the room.

39
00:02:17.251 --> 00:02:20.353
"I hope so too," said Mrs.

40
00:02:20.393 --> 00:02:24.296
Maylie. "But I fear I may be in danger of losing the dear girl."

41
00:02:24.316 --> 00:02:27.818
"Surely there is no danger of anything so dreadful?

42
00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:30.460
She was quite well two hours ago."

43
00:02:31.528 --> 00:02:34.190
"She's very ill now and will be worse, I'm sure.

44
00:02:35.131 --> 00:02:36.592
My dear, dear Rose.

45
00:02:37.752 --> 00:02:38.993
What would I do without her?"

46
00:02:40.074 --> 00:02:41.475
Oliver did not know what to say.

47
00:02:42.416 --> 00:02:44.097
He could not bear to think of Rose dying.

48
00:02:45.758 --> 00:02:46.979
An anxious night passed.

49
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.441
The next morning Rose had a high and dangerous fever.

50
00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:52.983
"We must take action, Oliver."

51
00:02:53.283 --> 00:02:55.045
Mrs. Maylie handed him a letter for Dr.

52
00:02:55.085 --> 00:02:59.628
Losberne. "This letter must be carried to the market town, four miles away.

53
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.941
Then it should be taken by horseback to Chertsey.

54
00:03:03.882 --> 00:03:06.023
The people at the inn will know how to do this."

55
00:03:07.284 --> 00:03:08.705
Oliver put the letter in his pocket.

56
00:03:09.806 --> 00:03:11.727
"Here's another letter," said Mrs.

57
00:03:11.787 --> 00:03:12.927
Maylie, hesitating a bit.

58
00:03:12.947 --> 00:03:17.630
"But I don't know whether to send it now or wait until I see if Rose worsens."

59
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.132
"Is it for Chertsey too, ma'am?"

60
00:03:21.112 --> 00:03:22.633
Oliver held out his trembling hand.

61
00:03:22.653 --> 00:03:25.195
"No," replied Mrs.

62
00:03:25.255 --> 00:03:26.856
Maylie, giving it to him mechanically.

63
00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:32.439
Oliver glanced at the envelope and saw it was addressed to Harry Maylie at some great lord's house.

64
00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:35.141
"Shall it go too?"

65
00:03:35.542 --> 00:03:36.222
Oliver asked.

66
00:03:36.242 --> 00:03:39.244
"I think not," replied Mrs.

67
00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.565
Maylie, taking back the letter.

68
00:03:40.585 --> 00:03:42.807
"I'll wait until tomorrow."

69
00:03:43.868 --> 00:03:46.950
With these words she gave Oliver her purse, and he started off.

70
00:03:48.011 --> 00:03:50.893
Swiftly he ran across the fields and down the little lanes.

71
00:03:51.994 --> 00:03:54.496
Now and then he stopped for a few seconds to catch his breath.

72
00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.263
At last he arrived, hot and covered with dust, at the market town.

73
00:04:00.363 --> 00:04:03.105
Looking around, he spotted the large house that was the inn.

74
00:04:04.086 --> 00:04:06.287
He spoke to a boy, who had been dozing at the gate.

75
00:04:07.328 --> 00:04:10.550
The boy referred him to the landlord, who was leaning against the stable door.

76
00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:15.453
The landlord walked slowly into the inn to make out a bill for delivering the letter, and he

77
00:04:15.473 --> 00:04:16.534
was gone a long time.

78
00:04:18.315 --> 00:04:21.237
After the bill was ready and Oliver had paid it with the money from Mrs.

79
00:04:21.297 --> 00:04:24.820
Maylie's purse, a horse had to be saddled and a man had to get ready.

80
00:04:26.088 --> 00:04:29.491
Meanwhile Oliver was in a desperate state of impatience and anxiety.

81
00:04:30.612 --> 00:04:33.654
If he could, he would have jumped on the horse himself and ridden away.

82
00:04:34.795 --> 00:04:38.939
At last the letter was on its way, and Oliver hurried out to the innyard with a somewhat

83
00:04:38.979 --> 00:04:39.599
lighter heart.

84
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.164
He stumbled against a tall man, wrapped in a dark cloak, who was coming out the inn door.

85
00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:47.586
"Hah! What's this?"

86
00:04:48.006 --> 00:04:51.349
cried the man, fixing his eyes on Oliver and jumping back in surprise.

87
00:04:51.369 --> 00:04:54.472
"I beg your pardon, sir," said Oliver.

88
00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.482
"I was in a great hurry to get home and didn't see you."

89
00:04:58.642 --> 00:04:59.403
"Death to you!"

90
00:04:59.783 --> 00:05:01.384
cried the man in a horrible passion.

91
00:05:01.404 --> 00:05:06.448
"If only I'd had the courage to say the word, I could have been free of you.

92
00:05:07.388 --> 00:05:08.409
What are you doing here?"

93
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:13.392
The man shook his fist at Oliver and advanced toward him as if he wanted to strike the

94
00:05:13.412 --> 00:05:14.113
startled boy.

95
00:05:15.173 --> 00:05:19.036
But before that could happen, the man fell violently to the ground, writhing in a fit.

96
00:05:20.017 --> 00:05:21.678
Oliver darted into the inn for help.

97
00:05:22.899 --> 00:05:27.085
Once the man was carried safely inside, Oliver ran as fast as he could to make up for

98
00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:27.726
lost time.

99
00:05:28.626 --> 00:05:31.147
All the way home, he thought of the strange, menacing man.

100
00:05:32.148 --> 00:05:35.270
But once he reached the cottage, there were more pressing things to think about.

101
00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.331
Rose had grown worse.

102
00:05:38.491 --> 00:05:42.373
In fact, a local medical practitioner said it would be a miracle if she recovered.

103
00:05:43.254 --> 00:05:46.555
The next day visitors came and went, speaking in hushed voices.

104
00:05:48.356 --> 00:05:49.277
Late at night Dr.

105
00:05:49.297 --> 00:05:50.638
Losberne finally arrived.

106
00:05:50.658 --> 00:05:54.700
"There is very little hope," he announced after examining Rose.

107
00:05:55.132 --> 00:05:56.213
Oliver's heart sank.

108
00:05:57.313 --> 00:05:59.895
The next morning Oliver went out to walk in the fields.

109
00:06:00.935 --> 00:06:04.418
It was a beautiful, sunny day with a sense of peace and beauty in the landscape.

110
00:06:05.538 --> 00:06:09.681
Oliver turned homeward, thinking of Rose and the many kindnesses he had received from her.

111
00:06:10.721 --> 00:06:12.002
When he reached home, Mrs.

112
00:06:12.042 --> 00:06:13.303
Maylie was sitting in the parlor.

113
00:06:14.483 --> 00:06:17.745
"She is in a deep sleep, Oliver," said the grieving old woman.

114
00:06:18.806 --> 00:06:22.168
"Dr. Losberne says she will either recover or never wake up again."

115
00:06:23.356 --> 00:06:24.757
Oliver sat down next to Mrs.

116
00:06:24.797 --> 00:06:25.698
Maylie on the sofa.

117
00:06:26.559 --> 00:06:31.343
He held her hand, and there they sat for hours, refusing all food and afraid to speak.

118
00:06:32.384 --> 00:06:35.606
Together they watched as the sun sank lower and lower.
